    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Is this difficulty paying attention something you have dealt with your whole life, or did it start more recently?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Well, I suppose it's always been there. If I really think back... even in grade school, my report cards always said the exact same things. You know, they'd write \"daydreams\" or \"needs reminders to finish work.\" They used to handwrite those cards back then, on that thick yellow paper. Mrs. Gable was my third-grade teacher, and she had this beautiful cursive. They don't even teach cursive in schools anymore, which just blows my mind. How are kids supposed to sign a check? Though I guess nobody really writes checks anymore either, do they? Everything is just an app on the phone now. \n\nBut anyway, yeah, my mother actually used to have to sit right beside me at the kitchen table just to keep me on track with my homework. This was before I was twelve, I think. She'd sit there snapping green beans or peeling potatoes for dinner while she watched me. She always bought her produce from this little farm stand out on Route 9, the tomatoes there were just incredible. You can't get tomatoes that taste like that at the supermarket. But yeah, she had to sit right there just so I'd finish my assignments. So it's definitely not a new thing.",
